    <td>
<font face="Arial" size="+1"><B>An Interview with Aaron "Ahriman" de Orive</B></font><br>
		<font face="Verdana" size="-2" color="Black">By Jonric</font><p></p>


<font face="Arial" size="-1">Although the rapid rate of progress within the industry sometimes makes it feel like it happened at least a few years earlier, Ultima Online was actually released commercially by Origin Systems in 1997.  Other games can lay claim to being the first persistent online world, but there can be no real doubt that UO was the landmark title that very dramatically ushered in a new era for the entire computer gaming industry.  It demonstrated not only that "massively multiplayer" gaming was feasible with unprecedented numbers of players, but also that the subscription-based revenue model was practicable, and thus that it could be hugely successful, with unprecedented numbers of subscribers. 
<p>
Last autumn, the company announced an exciting follow-up title, thereby becoming the first developer to reveal plans for a second generation online world.  Initially referred to as Ultima Online 2, <a href="http://www.uo2.com/" target="_new">Ultima Worlds Online: Origin</a> also promises to set new standards for the genre with a plethora of innovations and features.  Among these is a strong backstory that incorporates a new Ultima world on a divergent timeline and two new races, the technologically-oriented Juka and the magically-inclined Meer, along with humans.  Recently, we had the chance to learn more about this aspect of the game in an interesting and informative Ultima Worlds Online: Origin Interview with its Minister of Fiction, Aaron de Orive, also known as Ahriman.
<p>
<b>Jonric: Please introduce yourself.  What's your role on the team?  What's your background as a writer and as a game player, especially in online worlds?  Do you have previous game development experience, and how did you arrive at your current position?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>I'm Aaron de Orive, aka Ahriman, the Minister of Fiction for Ultima Origin.  I'm in charge of crafting and directing all the fiction for the game. 
<p>
I worked for OSI back in 1989 in QA and played around with the original Wing Commander and Ultima V.  I've also played UO and EQ, but not for extended periods of time.  I also enjoy console games, such as Resident Evil and Metal Gear Solid.  The last PC Games I played were Drakkan and the most recent Dune, as well as, of course, Warcraft II and Starcraft.
<p>
But to be honest, I'm much more of a writer than a gameplayer, and that's where my focus really lies.
<p>
<b>Jonric: Do you feel any particular writers have significantly influenced you, and if so how?  What about other influences?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>Since I'm mostly a screenwriter, some of my influences have been Billy Wilder, William Goldman, Tom Stoppard, & David Webb Peoples.  Authors I enjoy are Alexander Dumas, Gene Wolfe, Clive Barker, J.R.R. Tolkien, Charles de Lint, and Charles Dickens.  
<p>
The films of Akira Kurosawa, Stanley Kubrick, Steven Spielberg, Ridley Scott, and Ingmar Bergman have also greatly influenced my work. 
<p>
<b>Jonric: What does your role o the team entail in terms of actual writing?  What types of writing do you do most often? </b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>I've created the history and cultures of the major races and all the new characters for Ultima Origin.  I've written all the intro cinematics, a great deal of the NPC dialogue, and a detailed outline to help guide the first year's events.  I've also directed the writing of the Prequel Fiction and the novels, as well as helped provide a synergistic vision for the world in terms of art, design, and fiction.
<p>
<b>Jonric: In case any readers are not familiar with it, please summarize the setting and backstory for Origin. </b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>The setting and backstory are really far too elaborate to summarize easily.  I highly recommend reading the prequel fiction at http://www.uo2.com/legends-prequel_0.html.  The ultra-short version is that there is a horrific Cataclysm caused by three different races in the past, present and future of Sosaria. Reality was literally unmade and woven back together again, with disastrous effects on Britannia.  UWO:Origin takes place in the world of Britannia about 200 years after the Cataclysm.
<p>
<b>Jonric: How did the Origin backstory come into being?  Who was involved in creating the basic outline elements such as the split timeline and the three civilizations?  What would you list as significant factors that inspired this outline?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>The foundation had already been laid when I came on to the project, which included the idea of a cataclysm uniting three different timelines and very basic concepts behind the two new races, the Juka and the Meer.  That had been the result of creative brainstorming on the parts of the core design team, which included Starr Long, Damion Schubert and Andy Hoyos.  
<p>
I crafted the specific histories of pre and post-Cataclysm Sosaria, as well as the cultures of the Meer, Juka and the Technocrats.  That process continues to this day, so I suppose it's taken me about 18 months to create Ultima Origin's fiction.   
<p>
<b>Jonric: Once the three civilizations were decided, how did you go about fleshing them out and developing their individual backgrounds and histories?  What if anything was involved in terms of research?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>One of my degrees is in History, with an emphasis on Asian societies, particularly India, Japan, and China.  I called upon these rich cultures for inspiration and found that I had way more material than I could ever use.  
<p>
<b>Jonric: What were the major reasons behind adding two new civilizations to an existing universe, albeit in a divergent timeline?  Has this created any major obstacles, either expected or unexpected?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>We wanted to live up to the legacy of innovation that the Ultima series had established.  We wanted to bring something fresh and unique to our fans.  It took a great deal of time and coordination to make sure these elements meshed with the previous Ultima fiction, but the payoff will be well worth it.
<p>
<b>Jonric: What are the principal influences and inspirations that have affected the development of the human race?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>The human race has developed into two different cultures: the Britannians and the Technocrats.  The works of Jules Verne, H.G. Wells, and Frank Herbert guided the development of the fiction for the Technocrats; the look was also influenced by such films as Metropolis and The Shape of Things to Come.  I took a lot of inspiration from Shakespeare's tragedies for the culture of the Britannians, particularly Romeo & Juliet, Macbeth, and Titus Andronicus, as well as the history of Elizabethan England.
<p>
<b>Jonric: Where did you draw inspiration for the way the Jukan race has been developed?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>Most of my inspiration for the Juka came from Japanese feudal society.  I also mixed in a good deal of the politics of Dune, the martial cultures of Ancient Rome and Imperial China, and sprinkled it all with a healthy dose of Chinese kung fu films.
<p>
<b>Jonric: And naturally, the next question is what has influenced the third race in the game, the Meer.</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>The history of India, particularly the Mughul Period, greatly influenced the culture of the Meer.  I also drew inspiration from Michael Moorcock's Elric series, particularly the culture of the sorcerer-kings, the Melniboneans.  For the look, I threw in a pinch of fantasy Anime, just for good measure. 
<p>
<b>Jonric: What are the most interesting aspects of writing for UWO: Origin, and why?  What are the most challenging aspects, and why?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>I've been very fortunate that I've been able to add to such a rich and successful mythos as that created by the Ultima series.  I get to work with some of the best folks in the industry and I get to be a part of something that will help set a new standard in MMORPGs.  The challenge has been in trying to figure out how to pack a tremendous amount of fiction into the game, which is how the Prequel Fiction and the novels came about.  We just have too much good stuff.  
<p>
<b>Jonric: In what ways and to what extent does writing for a persistent online world differ from writing standard fiction such as novels or short stories?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>The main difference is that I'm not telling a single story, with a beginning, middle, and end.  Instead, I'm creating an entire world, a setting that players will inhabit and interact with for years.  My job is to help them tell their own stories using the tools we provide for them.  This is one of the primary purposes of the fiction.
<p>
<b>Jonric: A cynic might suggest that the majority of players pay little or no attention to the background fiction or the backstory of online worlds.  What's your response to this?  What makes these elements important, and what difference does having depth make as opposed to just an outline?</b>
<p>
<b>Ahriman: </b>The thing that makes Dune and Middle Earth stand out above the dozens of other fictional settings is the amazing amount of detail and thought that has gone into these worlds.  These settings have legends and folklore, which I think we crave because they give us a common frame of reference.  They help define who we are, what we value and why.  Folks may not realize it consciously, but depth is a crucial ingredient to the success of any fictional setting, online or otherwise.  If it's not there, that setting will soon be forgotten.
<p>
<b>Jonric: Thanks very much to Aaron de Orive for these insights into the game fiction of UWO: Origin.  We certainly look forward to reading more of the background as development moves forward during the coming months.  
<p>
Richard Aihoshi - "Jonric"</b>
</font>


</td></tr></table>

